01:02:42,492 --> 01:02:44,132
(A student-led organization that organizes university events...)

1740
01:02:44,132 --> 01:02:45,632
(alongside the cheering squad)

1741
01:02:46,432 --> 01:02:48,762
(Sinchon's well-known band, Sonagi)

1742
01:02:49,362 --> 01:02:51,032
- Please perform for us.
- Yes.

1743
01:02:51,032 --> 01:02:52,572
- Will you? Sounds good.
- Yes.

1744
01:02:52,572 --> 01:02:54,842
Rain has come to meet Sonagi.

1745
01:02:54,842 --> 01:02:56,442
(Rain meets Sonagi.)

1746
01:02:56,442 --> 01:02:57,672
Rain is here.

1747
01:02:57,812 --> 01:02:58,972
Jae Uck is here too.

1748
01:02:58,972 --> 01:03:01,782
Jae Uck used to be a member of a band too.

1749
01:03:01,982 --> 01:03:04,312
We'll play the famous cheering song of Yonsei University.

1750
01:03:04,312 --> 01:03:06,352
- It's "We Love Yonsei."
- "We Love Yonsei."

1751
01:03:06,352 --> 01:03:08,322
- We love Yonsei - That's right.

1752
01:03:08,322 --> 01:03:10,052
It's been a while since I heard it.

1753
01:03:12,522 --> 01:03:13,862
- They're so young.
- Okay.

1754
01:03:13,862 --> 01:03:16,492
- This is crazy.
- We love Yonsei.

1755
01:03:16,492 --> 01:03:18,992
- We love Yonsei.
- We love Yonsei.

1756
01:03:24,932 --> 01:03:28,642
(It kicks off with an explosive sound.)

1757
01:03:31,412 --> 01:03:33,312
(Choi Eun U: Keyboard)

1758
01:03:37,812 --> 01:03:40,112
(Jang Ha Gyeom: Drums)

1759
01:03:46,052 --> 01:03:47,622
(Park Jae Won: Bass guitar)

1760
01:03:49,322 --> 01:03:51,532
(Park Ji Hyo: Guitar, Lee Jun U: Vocalist)

1761
01:04:05,742 --> 01:04:08,782
(We love Yonsei)

1762
01:04:10,512 --> 01:04:12,912
(At every Yonsei student's dazzling moment of youth,)

1763
01:04:12,912 --> 01:04:15,422
(this song was echoed with passion.)

1764
01:04:18,492 --> 01:04:22,692
(Sonagi, Yonsei University's band, keeps the roar going.)

1765
01:04:27,762 --> 01:04:29,762
(Bravo!)

1766
01:04:30,162 --> 01:04:32,832
- Gosh.
- That was "We Love Yonsei."

1767
01:04:32,972 --> 01:04:34,202
(Whenever Guests welcome them with a great performance.)

1768
01:04:34,202 --> 01:04:36,402
- That was amazing. Thanks.
- That was great.

1769
01:04:37,772 --> 01:04:39,542
- I love it.
- Wasn't it great?

1770
01:04:39,542 --> 01:04:40,842
- Yes. It was great.
- Yes.

1771
01:04:40,842 --> 01:04:43,882
- Let's sit down.
- That was awesome.

1772
01:04:43,882 --> 01:04:48,422
Five band members are here today.

1773
01:04:48,582 --> 01:04:50,452
Are the five of you all members of Sonagi?

1774
01:04:50,452 --> 01:04:54,162
As for the current members who play at the school events,

1775
01:04:54,162 --> 01:04:55,592
we have a total of 16 members.

1776
01:04:55,592 --> 01:04:58,262
We have 8 freshmen and 8 sophomores.

1777
01:04:58,262 --> 01:05:01,402
I see. I heard you are about to graduate.

1778
01:05:01,402 --> 01:05:05,332
Members are required to be active for two years.

1779
01:05:05,602 --> 01:05:07,802
- I see.
- And the end of our second year...

1780
01:05:07,802 --> 01:05:08,802
is around the corner.

1781
01:05:08,802 --> 01:05:11,172
You meant the end of membership, not graduation.

1782
01:05:11,172 --> 01:05:12,642
- You're right.
- You won't be active after that.

1783
01:05:12,642 --> 01:05:13,772
- That's right.
- Yes.

1784
01:05:14,182 --> 01:05:16,782
- They play until their second year.
- During the junior or senior years,

1785
01:05:16,782 --> 01:05:17,882
- you have to focus...
- That's right.

1786
01:05:17,882 --> 01:05:18,982
- on your career path.
- Yes.

1787
01:05:19,182 --> 01:05:20,822
How do you feel about wrapping up?

1788
01:05:20,822 --> 01:05:22,352
You must be so sad.

1789
01:05:22,352 --> 01:05:23,352
Yes. I'm so sad.

1790
01:05:23,352 --> 01:05:26,852
Actually, I joined Sonagi...

1791
01:05:26,852 --> 01:05:29,222
because I really wanted to play the cheer songs.

1792
01:05:29,222 --> 01:05:31,992
In fact, Sonagi is the band that is chosen...

1793
01:05:31,992 --> 01:05:33,032
to play the cheer songs.

1794
01:05:33,032 --> 01:05:35,662
So, we're officially done with the events...

1795
01:05:35,662 --> 01:05:38,132
where we can play the cheer songs.

1796
01:05:38,132 --> 01:05:39,432
- You're already done with those.
- Yes.

1797
01:05:39,432 --> 01:05:43,042
It's time to hand over the chance to the next generation.

1798
01:05:43,042 --> 01:05:44,442
So, I'm sad about it.

1799
01:05:45,612 --> 01:05:48,582
Do you have to leave the band after two years?

1800
01:05:48,582 --> 01:05:52,012
Not really. Juniors and seniors are called OBs,

1801
01:05:52,012 --> 01:05:53,512
- short for Old Boys.
- "Old boys."

1802
01:05:53,512 --> 01:05:55,482
- Are juniors considered old?
- When they're just juniors?

1803
01:05:55,482 --> 01:05:56,652
- They're OBs.
- It is what it is.

1804
01:05:56,652 --> 01:05:58,522
If juniors are the old boys,

1805
01:05:58,522 --> 01:06:01,422
- what about us?
- They're only 22-year-olds.

1806
01:06:01,422 --> 01:06:03,722
Honestly, it's... Well...

1807
01:06:03,722 --> 01:06:06,592
You played "We Love Yonsei" just now.

1808
01:06:06,592 --> 01:06:10,332
It means that they love Yonsei University.

1809
01:06:10,332 --> 01:06:11,602
- It depicts their love for it.
- Yes.

1810
01:06:11,602 --> 01:06:13,702
But when they sang "Yonsei" with us in front of them,

1811
01:06:14,702 --> 01:06:16,072
it sounded as if they were calling us old.

1812
01:06:16,072 --> 01:06:17,512
(Yonsei's different meaning, which is age, bothered him.)

1813
01:06:17,512 --> 01:06:18,742
They love how old they were.

1814
01:06:19,212 --> 01:06:20,442
I am so glad...

1815
01:06:20,442 --> 01:06:22,412
that our combined age is less than 200.

1816
01:06:22,642 --> 01:06:24,682
I'm so relieved.

1817
01:06:25,812 --> 01:06:29,282
How long has the band been around?

1818
01:06:29,282 --> 01:06:30,952
- The band Sonagi.
- It has a long history. Actually...

1819
01:06:30,952 --> 01:06:33,092
- Jae Uck knew about the band.
- He knew about Sonagi.

1820
01:06:33,092 --> 01:06:35,662
- It was first formed in 1986.
- Gosh.

1821
01:06:35,662 --> 01:06:39,062
It's still Yonsei's band with the longest history.

1822
01:06:39,062 --> 01:06:40,062
(A band representing the school with 40 years of history)

1823
01:06:40,062 --> 01:06:43,762
Many famous singers come to your school festival.

1824
01:06:43,762 --> 01:06:45,272
- Yes. That's right.
- He came this time.

1825
01:06:45,272 --> 01:06:46,272
- Yes.
- What?

1826
01:06:46,602 --> 01:06:48,902
Yes. I came and performed.

1827
01:06:49,642 --> 01:06:52,942
(Son of Sinchon came to the festival last May.)

1828
01:06:54,282 --> 01:06:56,812
(Returning home in glory)

1829
01:06:57,112 --> 01:06:58,412
- Yes.
- It was so much fun.

1830
01:06:58,412 --> 01:06:59,412
- Was it?
- Yes.

1831
01:06:59,412 --> 01:07:03,222
Rain has many famous songs.

1832
01:07:03,222 --> 01:07:04,422
Do you know them?

1833
01:07:04,422 --> 01:07:06,122
Yes. "How to Avoid the Sun."

1834
01:07:06,122 --> 01:07:07,622
That's a really old song.

1835
01:07:07,962 --> 01:07:10,832
It's from almost 20 years ago.

1836
01:07:11,132 --> 01:07:12,332
It was released 23 years ago.

1837
01:07:12,332 --> 01:07:15,662
- How old are you?
- I'm 20 years old.

1838
01:07:15,662 --> 01:07:16,732
(Born in 2006)

1839
01:07:16,732 --> 01:07:18,032
- How do you know the song?
- Exactly.

1840
01:07:18,432 --> 01:07:20,002
- What?
- He knows it, though.

1841
01:07:20,002 --> 01:07:21,502
- How on earth do you know it?
- I heard it in a drama.

1842
01:07:21,502 --> 01:07:22,972
- You've heard of it many times.
- Yes.

1843
01:07:22,972 --> 01:07:24,712
It's because we love music.

1844
01:07:25,172 --> 01:07:27,142
Everyone sang with him when he came to our festival.

1845
01:07:27,142 --> 01:07:28,312
We jumped and all.

1846
01:07:28,312 --> 01:07:30,482
- Then...
- Did he go shirtless again?

1847
01:07:30,482 --> 01:07:31,782
- Well.
- I'm sorry,

1848
01:07:31,782 --> 01:07:33,082
but I don't go shirtless all the time.

1849
01:07:35,022 --> 01:07:37,592
- Isn't it part of the deal?
- I mean...

1850
01:07:37,592 --> 01:07:38,592
- As an included option?
- Yes.

1851
01:07:38,592 --> 01:07:39,922
- It's not included.
- You take off your jacket.

1852
01:07:39,922 --> 01:07:41,822
- No, I don't.
- That makes sense.

1853
01:07:41,822 --> 01:07:44,062
- It's only when I get paid a lot.
- I see.

1854
01:07:44,432 --> 01:07:46,732
I remember him taking off his outer jacket.

1855
01:07:46,732 --> 01:07:48,132
- Yes.
- I knew it. You took it off.

1856
01:07:49,402 --> 01:07:51,532
- I took off my jacket once.
- Just your jacket.

1857
01:07:52,402 --> 01:07:55,172
- You all have different majors.
- Yes.

1858
01:07:55,172 --> 01:07:57,612
- What's your major?
- I'm a food and nutrition major.

1859
01:07:57,612 --> 01:08:00,612
- A food and nutrition major. You?
- I'm a computer science major.

1860
01:08:00,612 --> 01:08:02,812
I'm an economics major at the School of International Studies.

1861
01:08:02,812 --> 01:08:03,912
- I see.
- You're an economics major.

1862
01:08:03,912 --> 01:08:05,412
- I'm a management major.
- Management.

1863
01:08:05,412 --> 01:08:06,982
I'm a chemical and biological engineering major.

1864
01:08:06,982 --> 01:08:09,182
I see. I wanted to major in all of them.

1865
01:08:10,382 --> 01:08:12,952
(19-year-old Jae Seok had many dreams.)

1866
01:08:12,952 --> 01:08:15,262
- But in reality...
- In reality? I...

1867
01:08:15,262 --> 01:08:16,422
You dropped out.

1868
01:08:17,832 --> 01:08:19,462
Why do you bring that up?

1869
01:08:19,532 --> 01:08:21,432
In reality, I majored in something else.

1870
01:08:21,432 --> 01:08:23,832
You didn't have to mention that I dropped out.

1871
01:08:24,102 --> 01:08:26,272
While playing in the band,

1872
01:08:26,272 --> 01:08:28,672
what are the difficulties you face?

1873
01:08:28,672 --> 01:08:32,212
Actually, the number of cheer songs we have to learn...

1874
01:08:32,212 --> 01:08:35,212
- within a year is 51.
- Gosh.

1875
01:08:35,212 --> 01:08:37,382
- What?
- You can see them over there.

1876
01:08:37,382 --> 01:08:39,012
That's the progress report of this year.

1877
01:08:39,412 --> 01:08:41,922
- We have to know all of them.
- I see.

1878
01:08:41,922 --> 01:08:44,252
We learn two songs every day.

1879
01:08:44,492 --> 01:08:46,792
- During the first summer break, - Do you have to learn them all?

1880
01:08:46,792 --> 01:08:50,492
we came here every morning during the week.

1881
01:08:51,062 --> 01:08:53,532
We have to learn many songs, so it can be tough.

1882
01:08:53,532 --> 01:08:56,332
I think that was the hardest part.

1883
01:08:56,332 --> 01:08:58,332
Is there a professional singer...

1884
01:08:58,332 --> 01:08:59,602
who was a former member of Sonagi?

1885
01:08:59,602 --> 01:09:01,672
Lee Won Seok of Daybreak.

1886
01:09:01,672 --> 01:09:03,242
(Lee Won Seok, the vocalist of Daybreak, is from Sonagi.)

1887
01:09:03,242 --> 01:09:06,142
- Isn't Jo Ha Moon from this band?
- You're right.

1888
01:09:06,142 --> 01:09:07,142
- Right?
- Yes.

1889
01:09:07,142 --> 01:09:10,012
- He basically formed the band.
- I see.

1890
01:09:10,012 --> 01:09:14,282
"Sun" is a song by Magma, the band he was in.

1891
01:09:14,282 --> 01:09:16,722
And we use the song as a cheer song.

1892
01:09:16,722 --> 01:09:18,822
("Sun" by Magma won the Silver Prize...)

1893
01:09:18,822 --> 01:09:20,892
(at the College Song Festival in 1980.)

1894
01:09:22,422 --> 01:09:25,932
(Now, it's one of the most popular cheer songs of Yonsei University.)

1895
01:09:27,602 --> 01:09:30,832
Is there any cheer song by a pop singer?

1896
01:09:31,002 --> 01:09:32,732
- Yes. There are many.
- Are there?

1897
01:09:32,732 --> 01:09:33,732
What about my songs?

1898
01:09:34,642 --> 01:09:36,472
- We'll recommend it.
- We'll make it happen.

1899
01:09:36,472 --> 01:09:37,472
We'll make a request.

1900
01:09:37,472 --> 01:09:38,572
Which song do you recommend?

1901
01:09:38,572 --> 01:09:41,082
- What's good for a cheer song?
- What will be a good cheer song?

1902
01:09:41,082 --> 01:09:42,582
- "La Song" will work well.
- "La Song" sounds good.

1903
01:09:43,012 --> 01:09:44,952
- "La Song" is a nice one.
- You can arrange it.

1904
01:09:45,312 --> 01:09:46,312
Please play it.

1905
01:09:46,312 --> 01:09:47,982
- We will...
- I want to sing it with you.

1906
01:09:47,982 --> 01:09:49,222
How about "GANG?" Does it not work?

1907
01:09:49,222 --> 01:09:50,722
"GANG" has strong beats, so no.

1908
01:09:50,722 --> 01:09:52,252
("GANG" is eliminated because of its strong beats.)

1909
01:09:53,092 --> 01:09:54,292
Right. I see.

1910
01:09:54,622 --> 01:09:57,832
Anyway, we will try to get results...

1911
01:09:57,832 --> 01:09:59,732
that will give you a dopamine rush.

1912
01:09:59,732 --> 01:10:01,962
- We want to create good memories.
- Yes.

1913
01:10:01,962 --> 01:10:05,032
- I want to make a suggestion.
- What is it?

1914
01:10:05,032 --> 01:10:07,072
During the Yonsei-Korea University Games,

1915
01:10:07,072 --> 01:10:09,242
whenever Yonsei University scores,

1916
01:10:09,242 --> 01:10:11,012
we play a certain cheer song.

1917
01:10:11,012 --> 01:10:12,912
- Okay.
- It's called "Primeval Forest."

1918
01:10:12,912 --> 01:10:14,072
- "Primeval Forest?"
- I know it.

1919
01:10:14,072 --> 01:10:16,012
When you clear the round,

1920
01:10:16,012 --> 01:10:17,582
- we'll play the song.
- "Primeval Forest?"

1921
01:10:17,582 --> 01:10:20,052
- Sounds good.
- Okay.

1922
01:10:20,052 --> 01:10:21,422
- Please clear the mission.
- Good.

1923
01:10:22,082 --> 01:10:24,292
Once on a show,

1924
01:10:24,292 --> 01:10:26,092
I did the cheerleading.

1925
01:10:26,092 --> 01:10:27,792
- So I know all of these songs.
- I see.

1926
01:10:27,792 --> 01:10:28,792
That's right.

1927
01:10:29,192 --> 01:10:31,432
(What is Sonagi's present for Round 1?)

1928
01:10:31,432 --> 01:10:33,592
- You have pretty hands.
- Thanks.

1929
01:10:35,132 --> 01:10:37,132
"A uniform for the sophomores who shared ups and downs..."

1930
01:10:37,132 --> 01:10:38,632
"as members of Sonagi."

1931
01:10:38,632 --> 01:10:40,302
"Five long-sleeve T-shirts from Deus."

1932
01:10:40,302 --> 01:10:41,972
(Round 1 present: 5 Deus long-sleeve T-shirts)

1933
01:10:42,972 --> 01:10:46,412
(They're good for performances and practices.)

1934
01:10:46,412 --> 01:10:47,982
- I love this brand.
- Deus?

1935
01:10:47,982 --> 01:10:49,512
- Do you like it?
- Yes.

1936
01:10:49,512 --> 01:10:51,182
- Good.
- If that's for Round One...

1937
01:10:51,182 --> 01:10:52,382
- Gosh.
- Awesome.

1938
01:10:52,382 --> 01:10:54,182
(Picks on the Drum!)

1939
01:10:54,182 --> 01:10:56,122
(Flick the guitar picks...)

1940
01:10:56,122 --> 01:10:58,692
(to put them on the drum.)

1941
01:10:58,692 --> 01:10:59,722
(Round 1)

1942
01:10:59,722 --> 01:11:03,962
(Place 2 picks on the drum.)

1943
01:11:04,122 --> 01:11:05,632
- On the drum.
- On the drum?

1944
01:11:05,632 --> 01:11:06,692
(Put them on the drum like this?)

1945
01:11:06,692 --> 01:11:07,932
Gosh. It won't be easy.

1946
01:11:09,762 --> 01:11:11,802
Everyone gets to flick one pick.

1947
01:11:11,802 --> 01:11:13,302
It's too far from the drum.

1948
01:11:13,932 --> 01:11:15,072
(It's not even close.)

1949
01:11:15,072 --> 01:11:16,102
- It's difficult.
- Right.

1950
01:11:16,372 --> 01:11:17,772
- How...
- Hold on.

1951
01:11:17,772 --> 01:11:19,142
- This is...
- Should I sit like this?

1952
01:11:19,142 --> 01:11:20,272
What should we do?

1953
01:11:23,042 --> 01:11:24,082
Gosh. It's hard.

1954
01:11:24,512 --> 01:11:26,452
- It does work, though.
- You shouldn't place it there.

1955
01:11:27,622 --> 01:11:28,652
Do you have to do it like this?

1956
01:11:29,222 --> 01:11:30,382
Gosh. It's hard.

1957
01:11:31,292 --> 01:11:32,422
It's so hard.

1958
01:11:32,492 --> 01:11:33,692
Gosh. This is tough.

1959
01:11:33,692 --> 01:11:35,692
(Staring intently)

1960
01:11:35,992 --> 01:11:37,632
Can we move the drum around?

1961
01:11:37,962 --> 01:11:39,632
- No.
- Goodness.

1962
01:11:41,532 --> 01:11:43,562
(He's surprised too.)

1963
01:11:45,332 --> 01:11:46,432
- I got the hang of it.
- It might work.

1964
01:11:46,602 --> 01:11:48,502
I think I'll be okay with this distance.

1965
01:11:50,942 --> 01:11:53,972
I think I pulled it off because I was close to the drum.

1966
01:11:54,782 --> 01:11:55,942
(Yeon Seok, Rain, Jae Uck, Jae Seok)

1967
01:11:55,942 --> 01:11:58,212
- It's too far, so I can't do it.
- Exactly.

1968
01:11:58,212 --> 01:11:59,282
(The closer they are to the drum, the better.)

1969
01:11:59,952 --> 01:12:01,352
- You should do it over here.
- Should I?

1970
01:12:01,352 --> 01:12:02,682
- It's better to be close to it.
- Right.

1971
01:12:02,682 --> 01:12:03,952
What should I do now?

1972
01:12:03,952 --> 01:12:06,292
(Following Jae Uck's advice, he moves.)

1973
01:12:06,292 --> 01:12:08,062
(Crammed together)

1974
01:12:08,062 --> 01:12:09,222
- It's too far, isn't it?
- Yes, it is.

1975
01:12:09,422 --> 01:12:10,522
You're too far from it.

1976
01:12:11,062 --> 01:12:12,262
I can do it with my left hand.

1977
01:12:13,792 --> 01:12:15,032
Yeon Seok. It's too far, isn't it?

1978
01:12:15,362 --> 01:12:16,402
You're too far from it.

1979
01:12:16,402 --> 01:12:17,972
(You should come closer too, Yeon Seok.)

